<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Hoya</h1><p class="st-meta">1202 CE – 1582 CE (approx.) · capital Nienburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Lower Saxon county on the middle Weser, ruled from Hoya and Nienburg until its counts died out in 1582 and their lands went to the Guelphs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Count Henry, of a noble family from the area, acquired lands on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> and called himself Count of Hoya from about 1202. His descendants extended their rule along the river between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a>, building a county around the towns of Hoya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nienburg-weser">Nienburg</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county lay in flat farmland and marsh along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, with river tolls and grain the main sources of wealth. It was divided at times into upper and lower counties ruled by separate branches. The counts were pressed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelph</a> dukes of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> was introduced in the 1520s and 1530s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the last count, Otto VIII, died in 1582, the county was divided between branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelph</a> house, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-luneburg">Principality of Lüneburg</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</a>. It later belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a> and is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig">